This is the 3rd try of exporting both enum and const enum in roosterjs. (#954)

Step4: Hook up compatible enums in API

## The issue

We were using const enum in roosterjs, however, exporting const enum is not compatible with isolatedModules mode.

https://ncjamieson.com/dont-export-const-enums/

One alternative solution is to replace const enum with enum. However, this will increase bundle size since compiler will generate bi-directional conversion code for enum and its values which do not need.

We need to find a way to support exporting both enum and const enum so that consumers can choose they one they want.

## Previous approaches

We have tried 2 solutions:
1.  Publishing two packages, one for enum and one for const enum
2.  Use webpack condition exporting
Both of these solutions have some issue when we integrate into OWA. So we need to work on the 3rd way.

## New solution

- Exporting const enum from roosterjs-editor-types
- Exporting enum from roosterjs-editor-types in a separate index file, and re-export from a new package roosterjs-editor-types-compatible as a shortcut
- Internally, we still use const enum since after compile, they will become just the values
- When exporting some API that accept those const enums as parameter, make it accept both enum and const enum. And we only need to check enum value since enum and const enum will always have the same values. This is guaranteed by generate const enum code from enum when compile roosterjs code.
- When use the enums from roosterjs code, always use "import type" to avoid pulling the bi-directional conversation code in.

For example, suppose we have a const enum and an API like:
```ts
export const enum MyEnum {
  Value1 = 1,
  Value2 = 2,
}

export function myApi(p: MyEnum) {
  if (p == MyEnum.Value1) {
    ...
  } else {
    ... 
  }
}
```

After the fix, it will become:
```ts
export const enum MyEnum {
  Value1 = 1,
  Value2 = 2,
}

export enum CompatibleMyEnum {
  Value1 = MyEnum.Value1,
  Value2 = MyEnum.Value2,
}

import {MyEnum} from 'roosterjs-editor-types';
import {CompatibleMyEnum} from 'roosterjs-editor-types/lib/compatibleTypes';

export function myApi(p: MyEnum|CompatibleMyEnum) {
  if (p == MyEnum.Value1) {
    ...
  } else {
    ... 
  }
}
```

And when use it, both of the two ways can work:
```ts
myApi(MyEnum.Value1);  // Smaller bundle size
myApi(CompatibleMyEnum.Value1); // Compatible with isolatedModules
```

